WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.594
This walkthrough connects an MCP App to a
live Oracle database. The labeled local

2
00:00:04.594 --> 00:00:09.188
host uses the actual app bundle and MCP
protocols. ChatGPT connects to the same

3
00:00:09.188 --> 00:00:13.782
server through a private tunnel.

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.551
With the local app and private tunnel
running, open ChatGPT Plugins. Select Add,

5
00:00:19.551 --> 00:00:24.101
then Add custom MCP server. The blog
includes the exact launcher commands and

6
00:00:24.101 --> 00:00:28.652
the tunnel readiness check.

7
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.974
Name the plugin FinancialDB Notes. Select
Tunnel and enter your own tunnel

8
00:00:35.974 --> 00:00:41.948
identifier. This reopened form uses a
placeholder. Choose No authentication only

9
00:00:41.948 --> 00:00:47.921
for this authorized private tunnel demo.
Review the warning, acknowledge it, and

10
00:00:47.921 --> 00:00:53.895
select Create as a plugin. ChatGPT then
initializes MCP and discovers the tools.

11
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.524
Complete Connect FinancialDB Notes when
prompted. This is the actual installed

12
00:01:00.524 --> 00:01:06.048
plugin page. Select Try in chat, or
mention FinancialDB Notes in a new

13
00:01:06.048 --> 00:01:11.571
conversation. Ask to show notes and open
the dashboard, with no writes.

14
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.709
The Oracle Database MCP Java Toolkit owns
the database connection. Its YAML

15
00:01:17.709 --> 00:01:22.418
configuration references environment
variables, so source code and browser

16
00:01:22.418 --> 00:01:27.126
assets contain no database credential
values.

17
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:33.837
The read tool selects the latest fifty
notes. The insert binds note text as a

18
00:01:33.837 --> 00:01:38.674
parameter. Formatting the Oracle timestamp
as text keeps populated query results

19
00:01:38.674 --> 00:01:43.511
compatible with the Toolkit's JSON
serializer.

20
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.531
The TypeScript server delegates named tool
calls to the Java Toolkit over standard

21
00:01:49.531 --> 00:01:54.061
input and output. The read-only entry
point references a bundled UI resource,

22
00:01:54.061 --> 00:01:58.592
which compatible MCP App hosts can render.

23
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.759
The UI calls server tools through the
official MCP App bridge. It validates note

24
00:02:04.759 --> 00:02:09.518
length and requires its confirmation
checkbox before sending a write.

25
00:02:09.518 --> 00:02:14.277
Production authorization must also be
enforced on the server.

26
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:21.234
Configuration selects the OCI or GCP
profile from the repository environment

27
00:02:21.234 --> 00:02:26.468
file. Values are parsed literally. Only
the selected database settings reach the

28
00:02:26.468 --> 00:02:31.702
Oracle child process; tunnel runtime keys
are excluded.

29
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.366
Here, Add note was selected with the
confirmation box unchecked. The app asks

30
00:02:37.366 --> 00:02:41.731
for confirmation and sends no insert. One
specific conference note was then approved

31
00:02:41.731 --> 00:02:46.097
before the database write.

32
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.842
The approved note is now returned by a
fresh Oracle read after restarting the

33
00:02:52.842 --> 00:02:57.685
local server. This proves persistence
beyond the app process. The complete

34
00:02:57.685 --> 00:03:02.527
repository build and the MCP discovery and
resource checks passed.

35
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.939
The same app now runs inside ChatGPT
through the ready private tunnel. Refresh

36
00:03:08.939 --> 00:03:13.878
notes completes a live Oracle read. Both
modern and legacy MCP tests pass, with

37
00:03:13.878 --> 00:03:18.817
database credentials kept outside the
browser.
